/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  flowsheetDict --- ED08 tartrate stabilisation of wine, REDUCED to a
  potassium hydrogen tartrate model solution
  ===========================================================================

  THE OPERATION.  A young wine can deposit potassium hydrogen tartrate (KHT,
  potassium bitartrate) crystals in the bottle.  Electrodialysis prevents it
  by taking part of the wine's potassium and bitartrate out through a stack
  of cation- and anion-exchange membranes, and the cellar controls the
  operation by the drop of the wine's electrical CONDUCTIVITY, because that
  is what it can measure on line.  This case runs that operation the way a
  cellar states it:

      stack                      EurodiaED-100P-50;   // 100 cell pairs, 50 m2
      membrane                   CMX_AMX;             // the record names no pair
      targetConductivityRemoval  0.20;                // remove 20 % of kappa
                                                      // -> Choupo solves I

  (1) WHAT THE "WINE" HERE IS, AND WHY IT IS ONLY THAT.  The diluate is KHT
      dissolved in water -- ONE 1:1 salt, K+ and HC4H4O6-, nothing else.
      When this case was built (2026-10-05) the steady stack could not
      divide the current among a real wine's dozen ions: it gave EVERY
      counter-ion the whole Faraday share, xi I N / (|z_i| F), whatever each
      one brought, which stripped the minor ions to zero and left the
      diluate charged.  On ONE monovalent salt that arithmetic is exact --
      one cation, one anion, charge conserved -- so the wine was reduced to
      it.  The rule was supplied the same day (C35: the counter-ions of each
      membrane SHARE its current by z_i D_i c_i), and the thirteen-ion wine
      it unblocked is `ed09_wine_multiionic`.  This case stays as the
      reduction a student can check by hand: on one salt the shared rule IS
      the single-salt Faraday law, and nothing below moved.

  (2) WHAT THIS MODEL IGNORES, said plainly.
      * ETHANOL.  It is not in the feed because it cannot be: the stack sends
        every non-water component through the aqueous-species bridge, and
        `ethanol` declares none, so adding it REFUSES the run by name
        (measured on this case: `aqueousMapping: component 'ethanol'
        declares no aqueous mapping`).  The solvent is pure water, so
        ethanol's effect on the ion activities, on the solution viscosity
        and dielectric constant, and therefore on every ion mobility, the
        conductivity and the limiting current, is ABSENT.
      * EVERY OTHER WINE SOLUTE.  No sodium, calcium, magnesium, chloride,
        sulfate or phosphate; no organic acid but the bitartrate (no malate,
        lactate, tartrate dianion); no sugars, phenolics or polysaccharides.
        In a real wine those anions carry part of the anion current, so this
        model OVERSTATES the bitartrate removed per ampere.
      * ACID-BASE CHEMISTRY.  No pH, no H+, no undissociated tartaric acid,
        no tartrate dianion: the stack carries ions as they are declared and
        speciates nothing, so the pH change a real treatment causes is not
        predicted.
      * TARTRATE STABILITY ITSELF.  The engine computes no KHT solubility in
        a hydroalcoholic medium and no saturation, so it cannot say whether
        20 % is ENOUGH.  The target is the cellar's input, not the engine's
        answer.
      * The stack's own simplifications, as in ed01-ed07: no water transport
        across the membranes, co-ion leakage lumped into xi, membrane
        resistances measured in 0.5 M NaCl (the CMX_AMX record) applied to a
        15 mol/m3 KHT solution, a lumped electrode term, no thermal effect
        (the energy balance is UNAVAILABLE: the ion components carry no
        enthalpy datum, and the run says so).

  (3) THE PROVENANCE OF EVERY NUMBER.
      * THE STACK.  data/standards/assets/EurodiaED-100P-50.dat -- Vitor
        Geraldes' recollection of an industrial unit he operated, marked
        `origin measured; reviewStatus unverified;` (100 cell pairs, 50 m2,
        two hydraulic passes, 0.7 mm channel, design flow 3000 L/h); its
        three estimates (channel width, spacer porosity, Schmidt exponent)
        are ANNOUNCED on every run.  Its mass-transfer fit is the bench
        stack's, Geraldes & Afonso, J. Membr. Sci. 360 (2010) 499-508, used
        inside its own Re band (this run: Re 62.85, band 50-86).
      * THE MEMBRANES.  data/standards/assets/CMX_AMX.dat, cited in its own
        record.  The stack record names no pair, so the case must.
      * K+.  data/standards/species/K.dat (D0 1.96e-9 m2/s, cited there).
      * HC4H4O6-.  The catalogue record carries NO diffusivity, so this case
        carries a CASE-LOCAL copy, constant/species/HTart.dat, whose D0 =
        7.568e-10 m2/s is an ESTIMATE by Stokes-Einstein on a crystal-volume
        radius derived from the tree's own records, `origin estimated;
        reviewStatus unverified;`, ANNOUNCED on every run.  No measured
        limiting conductance was read, so none is cited.
      * AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTIONS, in those words: the diluate at 15 mol/m3 KHT
        and the concentrate at 20 mol/m3 KHT (round numbers, not a measured
        wine or a plant's bleed set-point); both flows at 3000 L/h (the
        record's design flow); the 20 % conductivity target; xi = 0.9;
        E_electrodes = 2.0 V; P = 2.5 bar; and T = 298.15 K.
      * WHY 25 C AND NOT A CELLAR TEMPERATURE.  The ion diffusivities are
        referenced at 25 C.  The limiting-current model corrects them to the
        run temperature by Stokes-Einstein, but the conductivity does not:
        it divides the 25 C D0 by R T at the run temperature, an engine
        inconsistency named and not fixed (it moves every ED golden).  At
        298.15 K the correction is exactly 1 and both read the SAME
        diffusivities, so the case avoids the inconsistency instead of
        teaching it.

  (4) WHAT TO READ OFF THE LOG -- every headline number can be checked by
      hand:
      * the CURRENT.  A 1:1 salt, one counter-ion per membrane, so 20 % of the
        conductivity is 20 % of the salt: 0.2 x 0.045 kmol/h = 2.5e-3 mol/s
        of each ion, and I = 2.5e-3 x 96485 / (0.9 x 100) = 2.680 A.
      * the CONDUCTIVITY.  kappa = c (lambda_K + lambda_HT), lambda = F^2 D0
        / (R T): 15 x (7.361e-3 + 2.842e-3) = 0.1530 S/m in, 0.1224 out.
        The two ions are equal in number and K+ carries 72 % of the current
        in the solution, because it diffuses 2.6 times faster.
      * the LIMITING CURRENT.  65.7 A/m2, and it is set by the ANION
        membrane: the slow bitartrate depletes its film first (the
        cation-side value is 170.2 A/m2).  At 5.36 A/m2 the stack runs at
        i/i_lim = 0.082 -- nowhere near the limit on this model.
      * the VOLTAGE.  U = 7.98 V, of which 2.0 V is the lumped electrode
        term and 5.98 V the 100 cell pairs; P = 21.4 W, 0.0071 kWh per m3
        of model wine.

  NOTHING HERE IS VALIDATED AGAINST A MEASURED WINE.  The golden pins this
  model's answer.  A full wine composition was run on 2026-09-19 and was
  parked, not shipped, for the reason in (1); DEV.md C4 records what a
  faithful wine case still needs.  The rectifier below the stack only
  receives the stack's electrical power on an energy wire.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        edStack;
        type        electrodialysisStack;
        inputs      ( diluateFeed  concentrateFeed );
        outputs     ( diluate  concentrate );

        operation
        {
            stack                      EurodiaED-100P-50;
            membrane                   CMX_AMX;
            targetConductivityRemoval  0.20;
            xi                         0.9;
            E_electrodes               2.0;
        }

        energyOutputs
        (
            { name power;  kind work;  expression "W_electric";  unit W; }
        );
    }
    {
        name        rectifier;
        type        electricLoad;
        operation   { eta 1.0; }
        energyInputs
        (
            { from edStack.power;  kind work;  target W_shaft; }
        );
    }
);
